---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Including Context - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="task-breakdowns" subChapterId="including-context">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        An AGENTS.md file tells the AI about your project. But when you're
        breaking down a feature into tasks, the AI also needs to understand the
        requirements and how the current code relates to them.
      </p>

      <p>
        If your team has adopted{" "}
        <a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first development</a>, this is where it pays off. The intent already lives in your repo: feature specs in <code>docs/features/</code>, and the constraints behind past decisions in <code>docs/decisions/</code>. A task breakdown then becomes a matter of pointing the AI at the right documents instead of gathering context from scratch.
      </p>

      <h3>The Bridge Between Roles</h3>

      <p>
        Task breakdowns sit at the intersection of strategy and development. The
        person writing requirements might not know how the codebase is
        structured. The developer picking up tasks might not have full context
        on why a feature matters. AI can bridge this gap, but only if you give
        it both sides of the picture.
      </p>

      <p>
        The simplest way to do this: run your AI tool from the project folder
        that contains both the documentation and the code. When your specs live
        in a <code>docs/</code> folder alongside your source, the AI can read both
        without you having to copy-paste anything.
      </p>

      <div
        class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap"
      >
        {
          `project/
├── docs/
│   ├── features/
│   │   └── todo-list.md          # What to build (the spec)
│   └── decisions/
│       └── 0004-local-first.md   # Why, the constraints to respect
├── src/
│   ├── features/
│   │   └── todos/                # What already exists
│   ├── hooks/
│   │   └── use-storage.ts        # Shared patterns
│   └── lib/
│       └── storage.ts
└── AGENTS.md                     # Project context`
        }
      </div>

      <h3>What to Include in Your Prompt</h3>

      <p>
        A good task breakdown prompt has three pieces: a pointer to the
        requirements, a pointer to the relevant code, and any additional context
        the AI might need.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="success" title="Example prompt">
        <p class="italic">
          "Read the feature spec at docs/features/todo-list.md and the relevant
          decision records in docs/decisions/ so the tasks respect those
          constraints. Look at the existing feature structure in src/features/
          to understand our patterns (especially how world-clock and
          important-dates are organized). Break the todo list feature into
          development tasks. Each task should be sized for 2-3 days of work and
          follow the existing feature folder pattern."
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        Notice what this prompt does: it points the AI at the spec, at the
        decisions that constrain the work, and at existing code for pattern
        matching, then sets expectations for task size. The AI doesn't have to
        guess at any of these things.
      </p>

      <h3>A Note on Time Estimates</h3>

      <p>
        When you ask the AI to estimate task sizes, keep in mind that it doesn't
        account for AI-assisted development. Its estimates assume a developer
        working manually, and they tend to be conservative.
      </p>

      <p>
        In practice, developers using AI tools are often significantly faster
        than those estimates, though how much faster varies a lot by task, so
        treat any AI estimate as a rough ceiling rather than a target. You will
        still need humans to provide the real estimates. They are more familiar
        than the AI with the codebase and have a better understanding of the
        complexity of the task.
      </p>

      <h3>Referencing External Documentation</h3>

      <p>
        If your requirements live in Confluence, Jira, or Google Docs, you can
        still include them effectively:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-3 my-8">
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            1
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Tool integrations</strong> - Claude Cowork
            can connect to Confluence and other tools through MCP servers. If your
            organization uses these, connect them so the AI can read your documentation
            directly.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            2
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Copy into the prompt</strong> - For shorter
            requirements, paste the relevant sections directly into your prompt. This
            works well for focused breakdowns of a single feature.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            3
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Local copy</strong> - For larger requirements,
            save a copy into your project's <code>docs/</code> folder. This versions the
            requirements alongside the code, and it's the first step toward{" "}
            <a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first development</a>: intent that started in Confluence or Jira becomes a durable in-repo doc the AI can always read.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Iterating on the Breakdown</h3>

      <p>
        The first pass of a task breakdown is rarely perfect. Treat it as a
        starting point and refine from there. Common things to adjust:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Tasks that are too large</strong> - Ask
            the AI to split specific tasks: "Task 5 feels too big. Can you split it
            into smaller tasks?"
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Missing dependencies</strong> - "Does task
            3 actually depend on task 2? What if we used a mock interface first?"
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800"
              >Wrong assumptions about existing code</strong
            > - "We already have a date picker component. Task 4 doesn't need to build
            one from scratch."
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-stone-800"
              >Vertical vs horizontal slicing</strong
            > - If the AI splits work into layers (all the types first, then all the
            hooks, then all the UI), ask it to restructure into vertical slices where
            each task delivers a complete piece of functionality.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        This back-and-forth is normal and productive. The AI adapts quickly to
        corrections, and each round gets closer to a task list your team can
        actually work from.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Remember">
        <p>
          The goal isn't to get a perfect task breakdown in one prompt. It's to
          get a solid starting point in minutes instead of hours, then refine it
          with your domain knowledge. The AI handles the tedious structuring
          work while you focus on whether the breakdown actually makes sense for
          your team.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Practical Tip: Edit the Output Directly</h3>

      <p>
        Once the AI gives you a task breakdown, you don't have to feed every
        correction back through the AI. Often the fastest path is to just open
        the output and edit it yourself.
      </p>

      <p>
        If a task description is slightly off, fix the wording. If two tasks
        should be reordered, move them. If an estimate feels wrong based on your
        knowledge of the codebase, change it. These are small tweaks that take
        seconds to do by hand but could take multiple prompt rounds to get right
        through conversation.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is especially true for things the AI can't know: team-specific
        terminology, internal priorities, or the fact that Sarah already built
        something similar last sprint. Rather than explaining all of that
        context to the AI and hoping it adjusts correctly, just make the edit.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Save yourself time">
        <p>
          Use the AI to generate the structure and bulk of the content, then go
          in and make the final adjustments by hand. Trying to get the AI to
          produce a perfect result through prompting alone usually takes longer
          than just editing the 80% draft it gives you.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
